NAME
    screen - turn the on-screen text console (glass TTY) on or off

SYNOPSIS
    screen on       mirror the console onto the GL screen (clears it first)
    screen off      back to a serial-only console
    screen          report the current state

DESCRIPTION
    With a GL card fitted, the BIOS CONOUT can MIRROR every console byte
    onto the GL screen as well as the serial port, so the OS and every
    program appear on the display -- the "glass TTY" of two-mode
    operation (see the two-mode design). screen is the switch for it.

    The mirror is OPT-IN and OFF by default (the flag GCONEN=0). The
    whole graphics ecosystem -- cube, paint, BASIC, the window manager,
    and the byte-exact GL tests -- shares that one screen, so the console
    is only drawn on it when you ask. `screen on` sets the flag and calls
    the GCLS BIOS to clear the screen and home the text cursor; `screen
    off` clears the flag and the console goes back to serial only. With
    no argument screen reports on or off.

    A full-screen GL program still SUSPENDS the console while it draws
    (the GTSUSP flag) and quitting back to the shell resumes it, so
    turning the console on does not interfere with an app that owns the
    screen. On a machine with no display, `screen on` is a no-op and
    prints "screen: no display fitted".

    The always-on console (mirroring without this switch) is a deferred
    piece: an always-mirrored console shares screen and card state with
    every GL program and collides with it in several ways, so until that
    is resolved the mirror stays opt-in. Proper scrollback is likewise
    deferred -- the console currently CLEARS and homes when it fills the
    bottom row.

EXAMPLES
    screen on       put the shell and command output on the GL display
    term            ...or launch the Term app, which enables it for you
    screen off      go back to the serial console

